            <ArticleTitle>Degenerative temporomandibular joint disease: surgical treatment and long-term results.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1149-58; discussion 1158-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">In this study, the long-term evaluation after surgical treatment of temporomandibular joint with manifested degenerative changes and internal derangement is described. The surgical procedure, including extensive exploration and remodeling of the joint, with interposition of lyophilized human costochondral cartilage, is described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty-two cases (37 joints) were evaluated at a mean follow-up time of 3.1 and 8.6 years after the surgical treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A significant reduction of dysfunction and pain symptoms was achieved in most cases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
